<!--Blurbs: The monks dwell without a fixed abode.-->
<h2 class="h1">Many<br/>
Sambahula Sutta  (SN 9:4)</h2>
<p>On one occasion many monks were staying among the Kosalans in a forest grove. Then, at the end of the Rains, they left to go on a three-month wandering tour.</p>
<p>And at that time, the devatā living in that forest grove, lamenting from not seeing those monks, recited this verse:</p>
<div class="verse">
<pre>“Today it’s as if discontent
is displayed to me,
as I see many empty seats.
They, brilliant speakers, learned:
	Where have those disciples
	of Gotama gone?”</pre>
</div><!--end verse-->
<p>When this was said, another devatā recited a verse for that devatā:</p>
<div class="verse">
<pre>“They’ve gone to Magadha,
gone to Kosala,
&amp; some to the land of the Vajjians.
	Like deer, 	wandering free from ties,
	the monks 	dwell without home.”</pre>
</div><!--end verse-->
<p class="seealso">See also: <a href="../KN/StNp/StNp1_3.html" class="sutta-ref">Sn 1:3</a></p>

      </div><!--end:sutta-->
